WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.279 --> 00:00:02.093
- Good morning Believe Nation, it's Evan.

00:00:02.093 --> 00:00:04.783
My one word is believe
and I believe in people

00:00:04.783 --> 00:00:06.550
more than they believe in themselves.

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:09.084
And my sincere hope is
that if you see in yourself

00:00:09.084 --> 00:00:12.365
what I see in you you'll be
able to change the planet.

00:00:12.365 --> 00:00:14.476
So to help you on your
journey today's message

00:00:14.476 --> 00:00:15.809
is be authentic.

00:00:16.664 --> 00:00:18.821
Over to you Oprah Winfrey.

00:00:18.821 --> 00:00:20.841
(rooster crowing)

00:00:20.841 --> 00:00:24.325
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:24.325 --> 00:00:26.441
♫ Espresso going to keep me goin'

00:00:26.441 --> 00:00:28.491
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:28.491 --> 00:00:29.905
♫ Every morning

00:00:29.905 --> 00:00:32.860
- I think that success is a process.

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:35.110
And I believe that my first

00:00:36.439 --> 00:00:40.073
Easter speech in Kosciusko Baptist Church

00:00:40.073 --> 00:00:41.742
at the age of three and a half

00:00:41.742 --> 00:00:43.516
was was the beginning.

00:00:43.516 --> 00:00:45.181
And that every other speech,

00:00:45.181 --> 00:00:47.043
every other book I read,

00:00:47.043 --> 00:00:50.601
every other time I spoke in public was

00:00:50.601 --> 00:00:52.376
was a building block.

00:00:52.376 --> 00:00:55.712
So that by the time I
first sat down to audition

00:00:55.712 --> 00:00:58.258
in front of a television
camera and somebody says,

00:00:58.258 --> 00:00:59.167
"Read this."

00:00:59.167 --> 00:01:01.239
what allowed me to read it so comfortably

00:01:01.239 --> 00:01:03.859
and be so at ease with myself at that time

00:01:03.859 --> 00:01:05.510
was the fact that I've
been doing it a while.

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:08.129
If I've never read a book
like never spoken in public

00:01:08.129 --> 00:01:10.430
before I would have
been traumatized by it.

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:11.847
So, the fact that

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:17.088
we went on the air with The
Oprah Winfrey Show in 1986

00:01:17.088 --> 00:01:18.760
nationally.

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:20.969
And people say oh but you see

00:01:20.969 --> 00:01:22.759
how you're so comfortable
in front of camera,

00:01:22.759 --> 00:01:23.592
you can be yourself.

00:01:23.592 --> 00:01:27.506
Well it's because I've been
being myself since I was 19.

00:01:27.506 --> 00:01:29.526
And I would not have I
would not have been able

00:01:29.526 --> 00:01:31.201
to be as comfortable with myself

00:01:31.201 --> 00:01:34.034
had I not made mistakes on the air

00:01:34.946 --> 00:01:38.422
and been allowed to make
mistakes on the air.

00:01:38.422 --> 00:01:41.650
And understand that it
doesn't matter you know.

00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:43.893
There's no such thing to me
as an embarrassing moment

00:01:43.893 --> 00:01:44.969
no such thing.

00:01:44.969 --> 00:01:47.552
If I tripped and fell,
if my bra strap showed,

00:01:47.552 --> 00:01:50.795
if my slip fell off if,
I fell flat on my face

00:01:50.795 --> 00:01:52.519
there's no such thing as
an embarrassing moment

00:01:52.519 --> 00:01:55.069
because I know that there is not a moment

00:01:55.069 --> 00:01:58.089
that I could possibly
experience on the air

00:01:58.089 --> 00:02:00.233
that somebody else hasn't
already experienced.

00:02:00.233 --> 00:02:01.756
So when it happens you say

00:02:01.756 --> 00:02:05.986
oh, my slip fell off and it's no big deal.

00:02:05.986 --> 00:02:08.661
I mean like I was on TV the
other day and somebody says,

00:02:08.661 --> 00:02:10.179
"Oprah, you have a run."

00:02:10.179 --> 00:02:12.359
Have you not seen a run
before in your life?

00:02:12.359 --> 00:02:14.645
Well I get them too, let me tell you.

00:02:14.645 --> 00:02:17.370
So I mean I can't be embarrassed.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:18.566
I can't be embarrassed.

00:02:18.566 --> 00:02:22.545
Now when I first started
out that was not true

00:02:22.545 --> 00:02:24.997
because I was under the
I was pretending to be

00:02:24.997 --> 00:02:26.069
somebody I was not.

00:02:26.069 --> 00:02:27.444
I was pretending to be Barbara Walters.

00:02:27.444 --> 00:02:30.038
So I'd go to a news conference
and I was more interested in

00:02:30.038 --> 00:02:32.205
how I phrased the question

00:02:34.533 --> 00:02:36.226
and how eloquent the question sounded.

00:02:36.226 --> 00:02:38.335
As opposed to listening to the answer.

00:02:38.335 --> 00:02:40.553
I so, which always happens
when you're interested

00:02:40.553 --> 00:02:41.752
in impressing people instead of doing what

00:02:41.752 --> 00:02:43.238
you're supposed to be doing.

00:02:43.238 --> 00:02:46.715
And it took me awhile, it
took me messing up on the air

00:02:46.715 --> 00:02:49.539
on during the live newscast.

00:02:49.539 --> 00:02:51.954
I was doing a list of foreign countries

00:02:51.954 --> 00:02:53.789
there was all these foreign names and

00:02:53.789 --> 00:02:56.988
then Canada was thrown in
and I call Canada Cannada.

00:02:56.988 --> 00:02:59.197
And I got so tickled that I called Cana,

00:02:59.197 --> 00:03:02.058
I thought that wasn't
Cannada, that was Canada.

00:03:02.058 --> 00:03:04.777
Excuse me that wasn't
Canada, that wasn't Cannada

00:03:04.777 --> 00:03:05.713
that was Canada.

00:03:05.713 --> 00:03:07.242
And then I started laughing.

00:03:07.242 --> 00:03:09.649
well it was it was it became a, it became

00:03:09.649 --> 00:03:11.754
the first real moment I ever had.

00:03:11.754 --> 00:03:14.197
And the news director later
said to me, "Well if you

00:03:14.197 --> 00:03:15.765
"do that then you should just keep going.

00:03:15.765 --> 00:03:17.813
"You shouldn't correct
yourself and let people know."

00:03:17.813 --> 00:03:19.962
I know, well who's ever heard our Cannada?

00:03:19.962 --> 00:03:23.585
So that was for me the
beginning of realizing that

00:03:23.585 --> 00:03:25.794
oh you can laugh at yourself

00:03:25.794 --> 00:03:28.596
and you can make a mistake
and it's not the end of

00:03:28.596 --> 00:03:31.267
the world you don't have to be perfect.

00:03:31.267 --> 00:03:32.872
And biggest lesson for me for television

00:03:32.872 --> 00:03:35.635
because then it didn't
matter, it didn't matter.

00:03:35.635 --> 00:03:37.356
Oh sorry, bra strap's showing.

00:03:37.356 --> 00:03:39.523
- Not being afraid to make
mistakes is one of the

00:03:39.523 --> 00:03:41.674
hardest things to learn how to do.

00:03:41.674 --> 00:03:43.917
And probably one of the most useful and

00:03:43.917 --> 00:03:45.619
important things that you can learn

00:03:45.619 --> 00:03:47.853
not just as an entrepreneur,
but as a human being.

00:03:47.853 --> 00:03:50.742
You know when I first
started doing YouTube

00:03:50.742 --> 00:03:53.105
I was terrified of making a mistake.

00:03:53.105 --> 00:03:55.547
I was terrified of what
you guys would think.

00:03:55.547 --> 00:03:58.501
I was terrified of putting
out an imperfect product.

00:03:58.501 --> 00:04:02.465
I was trying to memorize
lines and in my first videos

00:04:02.465 --> 00:04:04.838
they were really they were really fake

00:04:04.838 --> 00:04:06.546
it was a fake version of me

00:04:06.546 --> 00:04:09.714
because I was trying to
get the exact words right.

00:04:09.714 --> 00:04:12.693
Everyone knows Walt Disney for
his movies, and theme parks,

00:04:12.693 --> 00:04:14.277
and cast of characters.

00:04:14.277 --> 00:04:17.096
But most people don't know
is when he first started out

00:04:17.096 --> 00:04:19.643
he had no money, he had
to live in his office

00:04:19.643 --> 00:04:21.783
because he couldn't afford
rent for an apartment.

00:04:21.783 --> 00:04:23.813
And he survived on a diet of cold beans

00:04:23.813 --> 00:04:25.750
because we couldn't afford food.

00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:28.090
I would be sitting down
I'd be you know looking up

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:29.861
trying to memorize my lines.

00:04:29.861 --> 00:04:32.862
And when I realized with
much more practice was that

00:04:32.862 --> 00:04:35.385
it doesn't matter the
exact words that I use.

00:04:35.385 --> 00:04:37.389
It's more than feeling,
it's more the genuineness.

00:04:37.389 --> 00:04:39.704
And it's taking me a lot of
times it get through that.

00:04:39.704 --> 00:04:43.441
I've made 5,000 videos now
and I've been working on it

00:04:43.441 --> 00:04:45.438
and working on it and working on it.

00:04:45.438 --> 00:04:47.095
And I wish there were a lot more actual

00:04:47.095 --> 00:04:48.915
genuine moments too.

00:04:48.915 --> 00:04:49.883
And I wish there were more mistakes.

00:04:49.883 --> 00:04:51.172
I don't really make a ton of bloopers

00:04:51.172 --> 00:04:52.687
when I'm making these video.

00:04:52.687 --> 00:04:54.232
My team was always worried about

00:04:54.232 --> 00:04:55.715
walking through shots.

00:04:55.715 --> 00:04:58.455
And I'm actually excited
I think we got one

00:04:58.455 --> 00:05:00.497
where Alex was picking up a table

00:05:00.497 --> 00:05:01.854
and moving it out.

00:05:01.854 --> 00:05:03.602
Like I'm cheering and I'm pumped up that

00:05:03.602 --> 00:05:06.646
he's in the shot, that we made
a kind of blooper, a mistake.

00:05:06.646 --> 00:05:08.264
Did we get Alex in there?

00:05:08.264 --> 00:05:09.097
Yeah nice.

00:05:10.424 --> 00:05:11.674
Nice I love it.

00:05:13.262 --> 00:05:14.095
I love it.

00:05:15.306 --> 00:05:18.838
Even last month when my
audio didn't work properly

00:05:18.838 --> 00:05:20.969
you guys gave me a ton a ton

00:05:20.969 --> 00:05:22.909
it's probably the most negative comments

00:05:22.909 --> 00:05:24.549
in the history of our channel.

00:05:24.549 --> 00:05:28.056
Every video we put up we
got comments about the echo

00:05:28.056 --> 00:05:30.842
and am I in a cave and
people wanted to fly here

00:05:30.842 --> 00:05:31.826
to buy me a mic.

00:05:31.826 --> 00:05:32.994
And some of them were funny

00:05:32.994 --> 00:05:35.578
and some of them were just
super hate and negative.

00:05:35.578 --> 00:05:39.114
And I kept the videos up
because I make mistakes right

00:05:39.114 --> 00:05:40.499
because I make mistakes,

00:05:40.499 --> 00:05:42.384
my teammates makes mistakes

00:05:42.384 --> 00:05:43.875
we're still human.

00:05:43.875 --> 00:05:44.794
And I wanted to use that as look

00:05:44.794 --> 00:05:47.086
here's the channel that
has 800,000 subscribers

00:05:47.086 --> 00:05:48.474
and we're still making mistakes

00:05:48.474 --> 00:05:50.167
and we're still trying
to figure things out.

00:05:50.167 --> 00:05:53.804
And that's authentic for
me and so I think that

00:05:53.804 --> 00:05:55.464
the biggest thing is one,

00:05:55.464 --> 00:05:59.115
just realizing, having the
awareness to understand

00:05:59.115 --> 00:06:02.992
that you are holding
yourself back because of fear

00:06:02.992 --> 00:06:04.930
of judgment, fear of not being yourself,

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:06.600
fear of what people might think about you.

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:09.115
Most of the time we do things
that are so just subconscious

00:06:09.115 --> 00:06:11.285
and we don't take action on it.

00:06:11.285 --> 00:06:13.962
Recognizing that here's a moment right now

00:06:13.962 --> 00:06:15.722
where you are afraid to do something

00:06:15.722 --> 00:06:17.978
because of how somebody might judge you

00:06:17.978 --> 00:06:19.971
and then doing something about it

00:06:19.971 --> 00:06:21.923
and then taking action against it

00:06:21.923 --> 00:06:24.037
and then slowly building up that muscle

00:06:24.037 --> 00:06:26.082
so that the next time it happens

00:06:26.082 --> 00:06:28.473
you're able to expand your
comfort zone a little bit more.

00:06:28.473 --> 00:06:31.955
And just being able to
recognize that in your head

00:06:31.955 --> 00:06:34.612
you have this rational,
you rationalize it,

00:06:34.612 --> 00:06:36.180
you cope with all the
reasons why you shouldn't

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:38.505
be doing something but really
it's just that you're afraid.

00:06:38.505 --> 00:06:41.488
If you can get to the deeper
cause of I'm just afraid

00:06:41.488 --> 00:06:44.588
that's okay, I'm going
to push through this

00:06:44.588 --> 00:06:47.325
and I'm going to expand my
comfort zone and get stronger

00:06:47.325 --> 00:06:48.814
and stronger each time.

00:06:48.814 --> 00:06:50.895
Having that awareness and
then doing something about it

00:06:50.895 --> 00:06:53.509
will help you become more authentic.

00:06:53.509 --> 00:06:55.834
And it doesn't just help you in business

00:06:55.834 --> 00:06:57.445
people want to deal with people,

00:06:57.445 --> 00:06:58.493
people want to understand your story,

00:06:58.493 --> 00:07:00.716
people want to feel like
they connect to you.

00:07:00.716 --> 00:07:02.636
People connect to me
more now than they did

00:07:02.636 --> 00:07:05.215
when I first started and
I have the same message

00:07:05.215 --> 00:07:06.837
that I did when I started.

00:07:06.837 --> 00:07:09.019
It's just the way I deliver
it, it's much more authentic.

00:07:09.019 --> 00:07:12.105
And so if you want to reach
people and have them take action

00:07:12.105 --> 00:07:14.688
and grow your business,
authenticity is really important.

00:07:14.688 --> 00:07:16.993
You hear that message over
and over again on the channel

00:07:16.993 --> 00:07:19.955
but also just helps you as a human being

00:07:19.955 --> 00:07:21.308
to know that you can be yourself,

00:07:21.308 --> 00:07:22.517
be comfortable in your own skin,

00:07:22.517 --> 00:07:25.421
not be constantly worried
when you're out at parties

00:07:25.421 --> 00:07:27.815
or in social events of how
people might be seeing you

00:07:27.815 --> 00:07:29.059
and judging you.

00:07:29.059 --> 00:07:30.869
If you're if that's the world you live in

00:07:30.869 --> 00:07:33.901
when you're constantly afraid
of how people are seeing you

00:07:33.901 --> 00:07:35.797
then you're never actually
going to be yourself

00:07:35.797 --> 00:07:37.602
and that is not a happy place to be.

00:07:37.602 --> 00:07:39.685
And so that awareness to
understand when you're

00:07:39.685 --> 00:07:42.086
holding yourself back and then to actually

00:07:42.086 --> 00:07:43.598
do something about it,

00:07:43.598 --> 00:07:45.706
take the action even though you're afraid,

00:07:45.706 --> 00:07:48.156
do that consistently and
you'll see a significant growth

00:07:48.156 --> 00:07:50.805
not just in your personal life
but also in your business.

00:07:50.805 --> 00:07:54.391
So the question today is I'm
curious what is the thing

00:07:54.391 --> 00:07:57.721
that you have the hardest
time being authentic about,

00:07:57.721 --> 00:07:59.844
the thing you have the hardest
time being your true self

00:07:59.844 --> 00:08:01.602
about leave it down in the comments below.

00:08:01.602 --> 00:08:02.808
I'm really curious to find out.

